and perhaps it will for you as well.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:58.130
This is the most influential math book in human history.

00:00:58.530 --> 00:00:59.810
Euclid's elements.

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:06.022
For 2,000 years, it was the ultimate authority for absolute mathematical truth,

00:01:06.022 --> 00:01:10.210
an enduring symbol of exact reasoning from the ancient Greeks.

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:13.810
But the Greeks didn't approach math the way we do today.

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:18.530
Their methods fly in the face of some of the most important rules of modern practice.

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:23.290
But yet the approach that Euclid used in the Elements was the most

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:28.000
successful way to discover mathematical truth until the 17th century.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:34.730
Ruler and Compass Constructions. But, how could physical tools

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:39.840
like these actually be a reliable way to do abstract math?

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.351
Understanding what the Greeks were really doing starts with

00:01:43.351 --> 00:01:46.515
the question that anybody who's ever had to use a ruler and

00:01:46.515 --> 00:01:49.890
compass in geometry class has probably asked, what's the point?

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:53.010
What are these constructions actually for?

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:56.547
Most people would probably assume that the ruler and

00:01:56.547 --> 00:01:59.570
compass are basically a way of drawing nicer looking shapes.

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:02.530
Like they're a quaint way of doing it if you don't have a computer.

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:07.890
And while that's not entirely wrong to the ancient Greeks, it's far from the whole story.

00:02:09.010 --> 00:02:13.170
To them, diagrams weren't just incidental schematic illustrations.

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:16.610
They were part of the reasoning of the proof itself.

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:19.250
This is not how we do math today.

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:23.531
Today, every single logical assumption needs to be explicitly stated,

00:02:23.531 --> 00:02:28.008
like a line of code, and a proof isn't considered valid until it's completely

00:02:28.008 --> 00:02:29.730
independent from any diagram.

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:34.530
But in Euclid's Elements, the compilation of Greek math from around 300 B.C.

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:36.050
things are different.

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:40.102
In Euclid's very first proof, he makes what modern mathematicians

00:02:40.102 --> 00:02:43.810
would consider to be an unstated assumption, basically a mistake.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:48.130
To try to spot what Euclid missed in proposition one of the Elements,

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:50.890
you have to also know what his stated assumptions were.

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:54.490
Before he writes any proofs, he lays out some definitions.

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:57.427
Five logical assumptions called common notions,

00:02:57.427 --> 00:03:00.490
and five assumptions about geometry called postulates.

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:03.690
Whenever one of these is used in a proof, it'll show up on screen.

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:07.163
We can read this proposition as a recipe for constructing

00:03:07.163 --> 00:03:10.170
an equilateral triangle using a ruler and a compass.

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:12.270
You start with a line segment ab.

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:18.218
When you draw these two circles centered at A and B that each have AB as a radius,

00:03:18.218 --> 00:03:21.390
like this, then you get this intersection point.

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:24.710
Connecting the dots gives you another radius of each circle.

00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:28.990
And of course, we know that the radius of a circle is the same length everywhere.

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:31.950
So this is equal to this and this is equal to this,

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:35.470
since two things that are equal to the same thing are also equal.

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:38.830
Euclid proves that he's made an equilateral triangle.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.600
Did you catch the unstated assumption?

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:45.720
You never proved that the two circles overlap each other.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:47.240
This should have been some other axiom.

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:49.777
There should have been something else that said how we

00:03:49.777 --> 00:03:52.360
know when the two circles overlap and when they don't.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.920
But there's another way of looking at this.

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:58.814
Rather than making a mistake by relying on the diagram,

00:03:58.814 --> 00:04:04.278
the Greeks were arguably using this series of steps to build the equilateral triangle

00:04:04.278 --> 00:04:06.120
as part of the proof itself.

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:10.672
This is the perspective of Viktor Blåsjö, who hosts the excellent

00:04:10.672 --> 00:04:14.454
podcast Opinionated History of Mathematics, which features an 18

00:04:14.454 --> 00:04:18.410
episode season about Euclid's Elements and the history of geometry.

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:20.953
When people say there is a gap in the proof, you know,

00:04:20.953 --> 00:04:23.781
I think it is like an anachronistic way of thinking about it,

00:04:23.781 --> 00:04:26.290
that the existence needs to be ensured by a new axiom.

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:31.530
That's the way modern, you know, logic nerds think it's supposed to be right.

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:36.373
But in the spirit of the times, we should start by thinking of mathematical proofs

00:04:36.373 --> 00:04:40.980
as something that arose alongside the Greek tradition of antagonistic debate.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:44.051
You have somebody trying to prove a claim and they're

00:04:44.051 --> 00:04:46.740
confronted by a skeptic who wants to prove them wrong.

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:49.740
The prover is saying, now carry out these steps.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:52.447
The skeptic is then asked to perform these steps and had

00:04:52.447 --> 00:04:55.300
literally just drawn this in front of their very own eyes.

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:59.660
And then the skeptic is supposed to say, oh, no, I don't think they intersect.

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:00.660
There's no credibility.

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:02.620
You know, you just threw it, it's right there.

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:05.380
Anybody can see, you know, you just look, make yourself look ridiculous.

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:09.612
In the modern mathematics, you think of the skeptic as if it was like a logic

00:05:09.612 --> 00:05:13.817
machine who is just like crunching axioms, like a machine doing calculations,

00:05:13.817 --> 00:05:18.400
you know, and then, oh, there's no axiom here, error, you know, but that's not this.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:22.066
The context we must visualize, you know, we must think of the,

00:05:22.066 --> 00:05:23.840
the degrees we're doing this.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:27.480
If the skeptic wants to say, I think maybe they don't intersect, then

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:30.920
the burden is on him to give a credible way that you could doubt that these are.

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:34.658
But if you literally just drew it in front of you and it's obviously cutting

00:05:34.658 --> 00:05:38.298
right through it, there's nothing you can say that has any credibility to

00:05:38.298 --> 00:05:41.643
try to get around the fact that obviously they intersect, you know,

00:05:41.643 --> 00:05:45.430
like any normal person would accept. By following the steps in the elements,

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:49.190
our construction undeniably has two circles that intersect.

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:51.750
It's impossible to create this construction otherwise.

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:55.270
But how do we decide what's obvious in a diagram and what's not?

00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:58.585
I mean, we can't just look at this diagram and say the triangle

00:05:58.585 --> 00:06:01.590
is obviously equilateral because that's not a proof at all.

00:06:02.230 --> 00:06:06.550
So why is it that we can use the picture to know for sure that the circles intersect.

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:09.230
That is an inexact property because the drawing

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:11.750
doesn't need to be exact for the two to cross.

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:16.040
Obviously, however, what you want to prove is that this is an equilateral triangle,

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:18.470
that all the three sides are exactly the same.

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:19.590
Well, that's different.

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:22.790
Well, maybe one of them is 99% the length of the other.

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:24.070
I don't know.

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:25.630
That's not self evident.

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:29.470
Obviously that's something that needs to be established with rational argument.

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:30.830
And like Euclid does.

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:34.300
This is a key distinction for this interpretation of Greek geometry,

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:36.290
which is going to come up again later.

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:42.948
Because Euclid never establishes exact properties like equality based on diagrams,

00:06:42.948 --> 00:06:48.130
but he does allow diagrams to be used for non exact or topological properties.

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:51.516
Whether a point is inside or outside of a figure

00:06:51.516 --> 00:06:54.210
or the order in which points appear along a line.

00:06:54.850 --> 00:06:57.850
This approach to geometry allows the written proof

00:06:57.850 --> 00:07:01.090
and the diagram to go hand in hand to share the work.

00:07:01.730 --> 00:07:04.877
The parts of the proof that were impractical to demonstrate

00:07:04.877 --> 00:07:07.650
verbally are instead demonstrated diagrammatically.

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:11.530
So these diagrams, they're not just a supplemental

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:14.050
illustration to an otherwise logical argument.

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:18.210
They're actually part of the proof itself, which a skeptic would need to refute.

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:26.210
But for this to work, we can't just draw any picture and start doing geometry with it.

00:07:26.290 --> 00:07:28.530
Our diagrams need to follow rules.

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:32.830
So for every object that's specified in the elements,

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:36.470
its construction is given by a precise set of steps.

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:40.490
If the book says, let the equilateral triangle have been constructed,

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:43.228
then it's referring you back to Proposition 1,

00:07:43.228 --> 00:07:47.190
where the steps for constructing an equilateral triangle are given.

00:07:48.950 --> 00:07:52.379
From this point of view, an equilateral triangle isn't some

00:07:52.379 --> 00:07:55.750
perfect entity that floats in the eternal Platonic realm.

00:07:56.360 --> 00:08:01.226
It doesn't exist at all until you follow the steps to make an equilateral triangle,

00:08:01.226 --> 00:08:04.040
almost like the output from some lines of code.

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:12.656
But given that Greek mathematicians didn't write about their thoughts and feelings

00:08:12.656 --> 00:08:15.327
and were making inferences from their technical work,

00:08:15.327 --> 00:08:19.382
this is kind of a lot of inference to take away from one little proposition about

00:08:19.382 --> 00:08:20.520
equilateral triangles.

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:23.293
So let's take a look at what Euclid does next,

00:08:23.293 --> 00:08:26.920
because it's about to become very clear that there's more going on.

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:32.338
If the point of the ruler and compass was just to make accurate diagrams,

00:08:32.338 --> 00:08:35.220
then the very next proposition wouldn't make any sense.

00:08:36.580 --> 00:08:37.540
Proposition 2.

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:42.342
To place a straight line equal to a given straight line at a given point at an extremity,

00:08:42.342 --> 00:08:45.211
somebody has drawn a line segment on a piece of paper,

00:08:45.211 --> 00:08:49.540
and now you want to draw an equally long line segment somewhere else on the paper.

00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:54.260
Euclid accomplishes this by a very elaborate construction.

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:55.670
That's Proposition one.

00:08:55.670 --> 00:08:59.470
It involves drawing several circles and an equilateral triangle.

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:02.510
It's very elegantly indeed.

00:09:02.510 --> 00:09:04.070
This leads exactly to what you need.

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:06.510
The given segment has been reproduced in a new position.

00:09:06.830 --> 00:09:09.910
With exact mathematical precision, you can prove everything you know.

00:09:09.910 --> 00:09:13.341
The equilateral triangle, the radii and the circle are equal

00:09:13.341 --> 00:09:16.830
as all kinds of logical steps, showing that this is correct.

00:09:17.390 --> 00:09:19.846
This is something that I can literally do by picking

00:09:19.846 --> 00:09:22.350
up this compass and putting it down in another spot.

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:25.290
So Euclid acts as if this is not possible.

00:09:26.490 --> 00:09:30.330
One might say that Euclid behaves as if his compass is collapsible.

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:35.199
The compass stays at a fixed opening while drawing a particular circle,

00:09:35.199 --> 00:09:39.210
but as soon as it's lifted from the paper, it collapses or closes up.

00:09:39.530 --> 00:09:41.810
Of course, there are no collapsible compasses.

00:09:41.810 --> 00:09:42.970
It's not a real thing.

00:09:43.450 --> 00:09:45.930
Thus, AL is also equal to BC.

00:09:46.010 --> 00:09:48.383
the moral of this is that this straight line here,

00:09:48.383 --> 00:09:51.990
which was the one that I started with, is equal to this straight line here.

00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:53.070
It's all very Neat.

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:55.070
It's also very weird, isn't it?

00:09:55.630 --> 00:09:57.550
Seems totally out of touch with reality.

00:09:57.710 --> 00:10:03.034
If a craftsman or an engineer or an architect would need to transfer a length,

00:10:03.034 --> 00:10:07.470
surely they would not use Euclid's absolutely baroque procedure.

00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:11.870
So, what are we doing here?

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:15.690
If we followed these steps every time we wanted to copy a length,

00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:19.380
even the simplest diagram would be incredibly complicated to make.

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:23.848
But if Euclid's constructions are about serving a theoretical purpose,

00:10:23.848 --> 00:10:26.100
then things start to come into focus.

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:31.380
Proposition 2 becomes another verified operation that you can add to your toolkit.

00:10:31.540 --> 00:10:35.380
So each construction is like a little module or a little subroutine.

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:39.809
It's a bit of proof that signs off on the fact that this object can legitimately

00:10:39.809 --> 00:10:43.940
be constructed using nothing but the initial postulates and common notions.

00:10:45.130 --> 00:10:47.957
Euclid's version of this construction works perfectly

00:10:47.957 --> 00:10:50.410
for any situation involving copying a length.

00:10:50.810 --> 00:10:53.478
So it's not saying that you have to do this every time,

00:10:53.478 --> 00:10:56.195
but whenever we do want to copy a length, we can invoke

00:10:56.195 --> 00:10:59.930
Proposition 2 to show that it is, in fact constructible from the postulates.

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:03.770
But why did Euclid need to be this pedantic?

00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:06.610
What was it about the Greeks that made them so

00:11:06.610 --> 00:11:09.050
fixated on proving things in the first place?

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:13.849
Civilizations like the Egyptians and the Mesopotamians had been doing

00:11:13.849 --> 00:11:17.993
math in service of things like surveying land, collecting taxes,

00:11:17.993 --> 00:11:22.710
and constructing the pyramids for centuries, but they never wrote proofs.

00:11:23.270 --> 00:11:26.817
The first civilization to write proofs were the Greeks,

00:11:26.817 --> 00:11:29.590
with the first proof coming around 600 BC.

00:11:30.470 --> 00:11:33.510
For the Greeks, it wasn't enough for math to just be useful.

00:11:33.590 --> 00:11:37.547
They wanted geometry to address deeply philosophical questions,

00:11:37.547 --> 00:11:41.190
because at the time, philosophy was where the action was.

00:11:41.270 --> 00:11:44.309
And the Greeks had a culture of antagonistic debate,

00:11:44.309 --> 00:11:47.875
where the very nature of absolute truth was hotly contested.

00:11:47.875 --> 00:11:51.440
While there were people arguing for all kinds of wild ideas,

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:55.200
two main camps emerged that we still think about today.

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:59.260
The first were the rationalists, epitomized by Plato,

00:11:59.260 --> 00:12:03.040
who said that the only thing that can be trusted is intuition.

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:08.215
In this view, things like shapes exist in the perfect realm of ideas,

00:12:08.215 --> 00:12:10.800
untarnished by worldly imperfections.

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:14.829
And then there were the empiricists, often associated with Aristotle,

00:12:14.829 --> 00:12:19.090
who said that observations of the world are the only way to gain knowledge.

00:12:19.250 --> 00:12:23.410
But these conflicting viewpoints were doomed to endless argumentation.

00:12:23.650 --> 00:12:27.871
Their perspectives fundamentally came down to beliefs about how the world works,

00:12:27.871 --> 00:12:31.090
so one camp would never definitively win out over the other.

00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:35.663
But alongside this endless debate, mathematicians found a way

00:12:35.663 --> 00:12:39.890
to use constructions to answer the objections of philosophers.

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:45.679
It let them build a consistent intellectual framework which even outsiders could

00:12:45.679 --> 00:12:50.499
test and conclusively prove right or wrong, and it meant that their field could

00:12:50.499 --> 00:12:55.740
develop new irrefutable knowledge and leave those squabbling philosophers in the dust.

00:12:57.100 --> 00:13:01.003
Math, specifically geometry, would become an indisputable

00:13:01.003 --> 00:13:05.180
source of absolute truth in a world filled with uncertainty.

00:13:08.310 --> 00:13:12.973
But to build knowledge within geometry, mathematicians needed some starting assumptions,

00:13:12.973 --> 00:13:16.470
a set of ground rules or demands that you would ask of a skeptic.

00:13:16.870 --> 00:13:21.270
In ancient Greece, these demands were known as postulates or axioms.

00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:23.863
We associate these words with math, but their

00:13:23.863 --> 00:13:26.710
origin is in the crucible of philosophical debate.

00:13:27.750 --> 00:13:31.756
For Euclid and the ancient Greek geometers, the first three postulates

00:13:31.756 --> 00:13:35.190
in the elements were basic assumptions about constructions.

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:40.168
If we agree that the straightedge can draw a segment of a straight line,

00:13:40.168 --> 00:13:45.030
and that we can extend it if we need to, and if we also agree that a device like a

00:13:45.030 --> 00:13:48.604
compass can form a circle given a certain center and radius,

00:13:48.604 --> 00:13:51.709
then we've established our first three ground rules.

00:13:52.510 --> 00:13:55.247
It's crucial that any starting assumptions are simple

00:13:55.247 --> 00:13:58.190
enough to be plausible and are free from contradictions.

00:13:58.590 --> 00:14:02.430
Grounding the postulatin physical actions addresses these issues.

00:14:03.040 --> 00:14:05.600
That's what the ruler and compass achieves.

00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:09.105
It shows that Euclid's axioms are not just something some guy made up,

00:14:09.105 --> 00:14:12.360
which is, we know that people can make up inconsistent theories.

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:15.565
So with the ruler and the compass, we are now saying

00:14:15.565 --> 00:14:18.400
the we have geometries based on these axioms.

00:14:18.400 --> 00:14:21.280
Furthermore, these axioms are instantiated in physical reality.

00:14:21.520 --> 00:14:25.525
Therefore, they must be as consistent as the physical experience itself,

00:14:25.525 --> 00:14:29.920
which has a greater track record than human thought and than just imagination.

00:14:29.920 --> 00:14:33.500
Just making stuff up in your head has a fallible track record.

00:14:33.500 --> 00:14:36.140
Many people have failed at doing that perfectly,

00:14:36.140 --> 00:14:39.220
whereas direct experience is not full of contradiction.

00:14:39.220 --> 00:14:40.860
You know, nature does not contradict itself.

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:43.565
We know that our first three postulates are consistent

00:14:43.565 --> 00:14:45.820
because we can build them in the real world.

00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:49.492
As long as you only use the straightedge for straight lines,

00:14:49.492 --> 00:14:52.524
no measurement allowed, and the compass for circles,

00:14:52.524 --> 00:14:56.700
then any other necessary assumptions are implied by these constructions.

00:14:57.430 --> 00:15:01.350
Lines and curves are continuous because they've been formed by a continuous motion.

00:15:01.350 --> 00:15:04.230
And when they cross each other, they intersect at a point.

00:15:04.390 --> 00:15:08.712
The Elements goes on to build every single geometrical object that it ever uses

00:15:08.712 --> 00:15:12.870
with a ruler and compass construction, followed by a proof of its validity.

00:15:13.670 --> 00:15:16.790
So in a sense, the ruler and compass become like tools for

00:15:16.790 --> 00:15:19.910
building geometrical knowledge from the basic postulates.

00:15:21.590 --> 00:15:25.197
But if these constructions are meant to be embedded in the philosophy of

00:15:25.197 --> 00:15:29.005
Euclidean geometry, then there was a big problem that these greek geometers

00:15:29.005 --> 00:15:32.662
were going to have to reckon with, because it was well known at the time

00:15:32.662 --> 00:15:36.370
that diagrams could have subtle mistakes that would ruin an entire proof.

00:15:36.530 --> 00:15:39.395
If a mistake found its way into a geometric theorem,

00:15:39.395 --> 00:15:43.473
then not only would the proof be wrong, but it could potentially ruin the

00:15:43.473 --> 00:15:44.850
credibility of geometry.

00:15:45.170 --> 00:15:49.170
Because how can you trust a system of thought if it's been shown to make mistakes?

00:15:50.050 --> 00:15:51.090
Here's an example.

00:15:51.170 --> 00:15:55.890
I'm going to prove to you that some right angles are not equal to other right angles.

00:15:56.400 --> 00:15:58.640
Now, to be clear, this is completely wrong.

00:15:58.640 --> 00:16:02.080
In fact, Euclid's fourth postulate says that all right angles are equal.

00:16:02.080 --> 00:16:06.232
But in this proof, the reasoning behind every single step is flawless,

00:16:06.232 --> 00:16:10.800
except for one subtle, almost invisible mistake which ruins the whole thing.

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:13.440
Here's a square.

00:16:13.520 --> 00:16:16.800
It's got four equal sides and four right angles.

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:19.694
I'm going to draw a line over here, which I've measured

00:16:19.694 --> 00:16:22.080
to have the same length as the sides of my square.

00:16:22.550 --> 00:16:25.430
And then I'm going to connect this point to this point.

00:16:26.550 --> 00:16:30.230
So this, this, this, this, and this are all congruent.

00:16:30.950 --> 00:16:34.390
Next, I draw a perpendicular bisector to this side up here.

00:16:34.550 --> 00:16:38.310
And then I draw another perpendicular bisector to this side down here.

00:16:39.110 --> 00:16:42.470
Then I connect these points and I make two triangles here.

00:16:43.430 --> 00:16:48.070
This down here is a perpendicular bisector, which makes these two sides equal.

00:16:48.700 --> 00:16:52.300
Meanwhile, since it's perpendicular, this angle is also equal to this angle.

00:16:52.460 --> 00:16:56.060
And then finally, this side is shared by both of the triangles.

00:16:56.540 --> 00:16:59.500
So side, angle, side, these two triangles are congruent.

00:16:59.820 --> 00:17:02.300
So that means that this length is equal to this length.

00:17:03.420 --> 00:17:07.100
Next, I'm going to draw a line here and a line here.

00:17:07.580 --> 00:17:12.220
If I look at these two triangles once again, this was a perpendicular bisector.

00:17:12.300 --> 00:17:14.140
So this length and this length are equal.

00:17:14.820 --> 00:17:17.215
This line is shared between the two triangles,

00:17:17.215 --> 00:17:20.340
and it's perpendicular, which makes these two angles equal.

00:17:21.140 --> 00:17:23.700
Because of this, these two triangles are congruent.

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:26.900
And that means that this side and this side are also equal.

00:17:27.220 --> 00:17:31.660
Okay, so far, we have two pairs of triangles which are each undeniably equal

00:17:31.660 --> 00:17:36.100
to each other because they came from drawing these perpendicular bisectors.

00:17:36.580 --> 00:17:41.380
And that also means that this third side for each pair is congruent as well.

00:17:42.340 --> 00:17:45.851
So now, this triangle and this triangle also have side,

00:17:45.851 --> 00:17:48.340
side, side congruency with each other.

00:17:49.140 --> 00:17:49.780
Hmm.

00:17:49.940 --> 00:17:52.500
But this is a right angle plus some other angle.

00:17:52.580 --> 00:17:54.100
I'll call that measurement alpha.

00:17:54.340 --> 00:17:57.700
We know it's the same over here because these were congruent triangles.

00:17:58.020 --> 00:18:00.100
So alpha plus a right angle again.

00:18:00.500 --> 00:18:04.980
But this right angle is bigger than the other one because of this little extra piece.

00:18:05.540 --> 00:18:06.820
So what happened?

00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:11.801
Well, because my perpendicular bisectors are drawn slightly wrong,

00:18:11.801 --> 00:18:15.923
the diagram made the triangle on the right look like it's basically symmetrical

00:18:15.923 --> 00:18:20.560
to the triangle on the left, instead of being completely outside of the square like this.

00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:24.735
Since the right angle of the square is no longer inside of the triangle,

00:18:24.735 --> 00:18:26.480
the proof completely falls apart.

00:18:26.560 --> 00:18:30.080
This is why modern math is so stringent about avoiding diagrams.

00:18:30.160 --> 00:18:33.565
That single hidden assumption about where the triangle is located

00:18:33.565 --> 00:18:36.970
gave us a diagram that allowed us to prove an impossible result.

00:18:37.930 --> 00:18:42.170
But the Greek practice of ruler and compass constructions has an answer to this problem.

00:18:43.290 --> 00:18:46.379
Because if you use the recipes from the Elements to properly

00:18:46.379 --> 00:18:49.469
construct the diagram for that false proof, you would avoid

00:18:49.469 --> 00:18:52.970
the subtle mistake that I just made when I drew the picture by eye.

00:18:53.130 --> 00:18:56.168
Even if your constructions have a certain margin of error,

00:18:56.168 --> 00:19:00.202
following the steps from the Elements still gives you the precision that you

00:19:00.202 --> 00:19:04.183
need to determine these inexact properties, like which point lies inside or

00:19:04.183 --> 00:19:05.440
outside of which shape.

00:19:05.590 --> 00:19:05.910
Shape.

00:19:06.950 --> 00:19:11.243
In the context of Euclid's Elements, if a diagram like this hasn't been properly

00:19:11.243 --> 00:19:15.750
constructed, it effectively doesn't exist, or at least it can't be used in a proof.

00:19:15.910 --> 00:19:18.747
In fact, Euclid is very careful throughout the Elements to

00:19:18.747 --> 00:19:21.829
only ever use shapes that he's already shown how to construct.

00:19:22.310 --> 00:19:25.193
As he proves new theorems, he introduces new shapes

00:19:25.193 --> 00:19:27.510
that these new tools allow him to build.

00:19:28.230 --> 00:19:29.910
But this leads to some surprises.

00:19:30.230 --> 00:19:35.150
Shapes that seem like they should be simple, but actually aren't, for example, squares.

00:19:35.870 --> 00:19:39.743
If you asked me to construct a square using Euclid's propositions and I didn't know

00:19:39.743 --> 00:19:43.710
any better, I'd probably say the simplest way to do it would be something like this.

00:19:44.110 --> 00:19:45.230
Draw a line segment.

00:19:45.230 --> 00:19:49.739
Construct another line segment equal to the first and perpendicular to the first,

00:19:49.739 --> 00:19:53.302
using Proposition 11, and then just do the same thing again and

00:19:53.302 --> 00:19:56.030
then again one more time, and you have a square.

00:19:58.270 --> 00:20:02.050
But when it comes time to prove that this really is a square, we've got a problem.

00:20:02.370 --> 00:20:06.683
Because to be sure that the side opposite the first one really is the same

00:20:06.683 --> 00:20:11.170
length as the original, we would need to say something about parallel lines.

00:20:11.810 --> 00:20:16.914
Euclid waits until more than halfway through book one before he finally uses his

00:20:16.914 --> 00:20:22.210
most famous and most controversial postulate, postulate 5, the parallel postulate.

00:20:23.170 --> 00:20:26.690
Unlike the other postulates, it's not simple or intuitive.

00:20:27.280 --> 00:20:30.240
Even the actual statement is strangely convoluted.

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:35.126
"If a straight line falling across two other straight lines makes internal angles

00:20:35.126 --> 00:20:38.879
on the same side of itself, whose sum is less than two right angles,

00:20:38.879 --> 00:20:43.339
then the two other straight lines being produced to infinity meet on that side of

00:20:43.339 --> 00:20:47.744
the original straight line, that the sum of the internal angles is less than two

00:20:47.744 --> 00:20:48.560
right angles."

00:20:49.360 --> 00:20:52.080
This is a statement about non parallel lines.

00:20:52.080 --> 00:20:56.930
But taking the contrapositive gives logically equivalent information about parallels.

00:20:57.250 --> 00:21:02.450
Anytime two lines don't cross, then the sum of the angles equals 180 degrees.

00:21:02.770 --> 00:21:07.096
Today, we usually say it like for any line and a point not on the line,

00:21:07.096 --> 00:21:11.970
there's exactly one line through the point which is parallel to the first line.

00:21:12.530 --> 00:21:16.088
But in the context of ruler and compass constructions,

00:21:16.088 --> 00:21:19.252
the first one is not the same as the other two,

00:21:19.252 --> 00:21:23.469
because there's no way to physically verify that lines continue

00:21:23.469 --> 00:21:25.380
forever and never intersect.

00:21:25.860 --> 00:21:27.380
How do you check for never?

00:21:28.260 --> 00:21:32.918
Even though Euclid's definitions say that parallel lines are two lines that never

00:21:32.918 --> 00:21:37.632
intersect, when it comes time to ask somebody to accept an assumption about them,

00:21:37.632 --> 00:21:42.233
he avoids the problem of checking for never by phrasing it in a way that can be

00:21:42.233 --> 00:21:42.980
constructed.

00:21:44.100 --> 00:21:47.925
Whenever two lines are drawn through a transversal so that they're converging,

00:21:47.925 --> 00:21:49.200
they'll cross eventually.

00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:52.548
It's an assertion that's physically checkable,

00:21:52.548 --> 00:21:56.080
even if doing it in practice requires a bit of extra space.

00:21:57.040 --> 00:22:01.949
But even so, this is not empirical science, because this postulate lets Euclid

00:22:01.949 --> 00:22:06.480
derive theorems about parallel lines that are not physically checkable.

00:22:06.960 --> 00:22:10.261
We can't actually make lines that go on forever without touching,

00:22:10.261 --> 00:22:12.800
especially on the spherical surface of the Earth.

00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:17.453
So Euclid is is using physically verifiable principles to develop

00:22:17.453 --> 00:22:21.280
a theory that goes beyond the scope of empirical observation.

00:22:21.680 --> 00:22:24.632
It's an approach that's different from both Platonic

00:22:24.632 --> 00:22:26.960
rationalism and Aristotelian empiricism.

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:31.976
And once he introduces the parallel postulate for the first time in Proposition 29,

00:22:31.976 --> 00:22:36.480
all of the subsequent theorems and constructions require it for their existence.

00:22:36.800 --> 00:22:39.949
This kind of additive structure of the elements reveals hidden

00:22:39.949 --> 00:22:43.200
complexities in shapes that we might have assumed to be simple.

00:22:43.770 --> 00:22:47.530
So Euclid's construction of a square is much more complicated than mine.

00:22:47.690 --> 00:22:51.756
In fact, when I followed his construction and I included all the subroutines

00:22:51.756 --> 00:22:56.090
from earlier propositions, it took me close to 10 minutes just to make a square.

