00:24:47.620 --> 00:24:52.448
So even though you made sure you had right angles at the base of the quadrilateral,

00:24:52.448 --> 00:24:56.577
and that the perpendicular sides were equal, the fourth and final side

00:24:56.577 --> 00:24:59.660
somehow still manages to miss the mark, so to speak.

00:24:59.660 --> 00:25:01.300
It makes non right angles.

00:25:01.460 --> 00:25:04.020
It's as if the sides are sort of bent.

00:25:04.420 --> 00:25:08.420
It's not that Greek mathematicians were doubting whether or not squares existed.

00:25:08.420 --> 00:25:13.460
Or that Euclid somehow anticipated non Euclidean geometry, which he didn't.

00:25:13.460 --> 00:25:18.599
But rather it's that Euclid was using the process of constructions combined with

00:25:18.599 --> 00:25:23.995
axiomatic deductive proofs to pin down the very foundations of geometric knowledge.

00:25:23.995 --> 00:25:29.134
To systematically catalogue every assumption that's required before we can make

00:25:29.134 --> 00:25:30.740
something like a square.

00:25:31.330 --> 00:25:34.665
So the Elements isn't just a collection of proofs accompanied

00:25:34.665 --> 00:25:37.890
by subroutines showing how to construct geometric objects.

00:25:38.450 --> 00:25:43.890
It's a taxonomy of the exact components that are required for any geometric object.

00:25:44.370 --> 00:25:47.330
A family tree of the universe of geometry.

00:25:47.410 --> 00:25:52.807
And with this type of analysis, Euclid is able to identify precisely what fundamental

00:25:52.807 --> 00:25:57.570
starting assumptions are required for the entire Greek system of geometry.

00:25:58.090 --> 00:26:01.792
It's not obvious that the square requires the parallel postulate

00:26:01.792 --> 00:26:05.610
to be true until you pull it apart and put it under a microscope.

